WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Settle in a position that feels steady and awake.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.045
Keep the eyes open if a wider view helps you stay present.

3
00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:19.610
Notice where you are, the time of day, and the surface supporting you.

4
00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:27.000
You remain free to move, narrow your attention, or end the practice.

5
00:00:34.273 --> 00:00:36.513
Begin with one clear anchor.

6
00:00:38.213 --> 00:00:44.518
Feel the feet, the seat, the hands, or one ordinary breath.

7
00:00:46.218 --> 00:00:50.818
Let attention rest there without holding tightly.

8
00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:58.913
This anchor will remain available whenever the wider field feels busy or unclear.

9
00:01:11.034 --> 00:01:12.474
Now include sounds.

10
00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:27.028
Let hearing receive whatever is already arriving: a close sound, a distant sound, a steady sound, or the space between sounds.

11
00:01:28.728 --> 00:01:32.574
You do not need to name the source.

12
00:01:34.274 --> 00:01:42.208
Notice that each sound appears, changes, and is no longer the same.

13
00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:05.110
Keep hearing open and include sensation in the body.

14
00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:15.385
Pressure, temperature, movement, stillness, comfort, or discomfort may be present.

15
00:02:17.085 --> 00:02:21.505
Let sensations occupy their own places.

16
00:02:23.205 --> 00:02:29.335
There is no need to combine them into one story about how the body feels.

17
00:02:47.516 --> 00:02:49.396
Return briefly to the anchor.

18
00:02:51.096 --> 00:02:52.936
One point of contact.

19
00:02:54.636 --> 00:02:55.836
One breath.

20
00:02:57.536 --> 00:03:01.531
Then allow the field to widen again.

21
00:03:10.622 --> 00:03:15.872
Notice how attention chooses something from the field.

22
00:03:17.572 --> 00:03:21.707
A sound becomes prominent, then a sensation.

23
00:03:23.407 --> 00:03:26.682
A thought may move to the front.

24
00:03:28.382 --> 00:03:33.872
You do not have to prevent this movement.

25
00:03:35.572 --> 00:03:41.502
Recognize what is foreground now, and what remains quietly in the background.

26
00:04:02.714 --> 00:04:07.054
Thoughts can be included as events too.

27
00:04:08.754 --> 00:04:17.674
You may notice words, pictures, memories, plans, or fragments that do not form a complete idea.

28
00:04:19.374 --> 00:04:26.974
Rather than entering the subject, notice the simple fact that thinking is happening.

29
00:04:28.674 --> 00:04:30.314
Then listen again.

30
00:04:32.014 --> 00:04:33.934
Feel the body again.

31
00:04:55.145 --> 00:05:02.815
If a thought or feeling becomes too strong, make the field smaller.

32
00:05:04.515 --> 00:05:14.690
Open your eyes, name three visible objects, and feel both feet or another reliable support.

33
00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:22.245
Open awareness is a choice, not a demand to stay with something overwhelming.

34
00:05:34.366 --> 00:05:42.386
When it feels workable, let sounds, sensations, and thoughts share the same space.

35
00:05:44.086 --> 00:05:53.081
Nothing has to be pushed away, and nothing has to receive all of your attention.

36
00:05:54.781 --> 00:05:59.536
Experience can come and go within a larger awareness.

37
00:06:26.808 --> 00:06:29.168
Notice a moment of change.

38
00:06:30.868 --> 00:06:32.068
A sound ends.

39
00:06:33.768 --> 00:06:35.048
An itch moves.

40
00:06:36.748 --> 00:06:38.428
A breath turns.

41
00:06:40.128 --> 00:06:42.408
A thought loses its edge.

42
00:06:44.108 --> 00:06:48.023
You do not need to make change happen.

43
00:06:49.723 --> 00:06:53.913
The field is already changing on its own.

44
00:07:15.125 --> 00:07:30.405
See whether there is also something steady: the weight of the body, the general presence of the room, or the simple capacity to notice.

45
00:07:32.105 --> 00:07:37.355
Let changing events and steady support be known together.

46
00:08:01.597 --> 00:08:05.607
Release any effort to monitor everything.

47
00:08:07.307 --> 00:08:11.812
Open awareness does not mean tracking every detail.

48
00:08:13.512 --> 00:08:16.232
Let most experience remain unexamined.

49
00:08:17.932 --> 00:08:23.467
Receive what becomes clear, and allow it to pass back into the wider field.

50
00:08:50.739 --> 00:08:55.949
For the next moments, notice only when something captures attention.

51
00:08:57.649 --> 00:09:02.054
Silently acknowledge hearing, feeling, or thinking.

52
00:09:03.754 --> 00:09:05.474
Then leave the label behind.

53
00:09:07.174 --> 00:09:11.529
There is no need to label the same event twice.

54
00:09:41.831 --> 00:09:44.231
Now stop using labels.

55
00:09:45.931 --> 00:09:59.766
Rest as best you can with the whole field: body supported, air around you, sounds arriving, breath moving, thoughts appearing.

56
00:10:01.466 --> 00:10:07.201
If you become lost, return to the anchor and widen again when ready.

57
00:10:43.564 --> 00:10:48.014
Begin to choose rather than simply receive.

58
00:10:49.714 --> 00:10:51.994
Select one sound and listen.

59
00:10:53.694 --> 00:10:57.744
Select one contact point and feel it.

60
00:10:59.444 --> 00:11:03.239
Select the next breath and accompany it.

61
00:11:04.939 --> 00:11:10.849
Notice that attention can be open and can also gather deliberately.

62
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.640
Let the eyes focus clearly.

63
00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.900
Take in colors and edges.

64
00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:41.210
Feel the body in its full position and make any small movement that is needed.

65
00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:53.120
The wider field remains available as you continue, but you do not have to carry all of it.

66
00:11:54.820 --> 00:12:00.000
Meet the next thing while remembering there is space around it.
